(Ledger batch VAL-A: the eight `value` leaves (docs/ledger/values.md rows
 VAL-03, 05, 06, 07, 09, 10, 11, 13).

 Each leaf is emitted on its own into one program, which is then COMPILED
 AND RUN by the real compiler. Checking the emitted text alone would miss
 the only thing these leaves exist for - that their assertions hold - so
 the program ends `Exit 0.` and any other status is either a ledger
 assertion that fired (95) or a crash.

 Emitted twice from the same seed and compared, because the leaves spend
 names out of a shared vocabulary: if that record ever outlives a program,
 two programs from one seed stop being identical and every campaign's
 reproducibility goes with it.)

To 'emit the value leaves'.
    clear gen_out.
    'gen reset value names'.
    'gen leaf value dispatch' of "" and false.
    'gen leaf value return' of "" and false.
    'gen leaf value name' of "" and false.
    'gen leaf value retag' of "" and false.
    'gen leaf value retype' of "" and false.
    'gen leaf value retype sum' of "" and false.
    'gen leaf value predicate' of "" and false.
    'gen leaf value bad retype' of "" and false.
    'gen emit' of "Exit 0.\n".

'rng seed' of 7.
'emit the value leaves'.
a text called 'the first emission' is 'gen text'.
'gen write' of "./vf_values_a.vox".

'rng seed' of 7.
'emit the value leaves'.
a text called 'the second emission' is 'gen text'.
Print "reproducible: {'the first emission' is equal to 'the second emission'}".

a boolean called 'the dispatch row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-03".
Print "VAL-03 asserts: {'the dispatch row is covered'}".
a boolean called 'the return row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-05".
Print "VAL-05 asserts: {'the return row is covered'}".
a boolean called 'the name row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-06".
Print "VAL-06 asserts: {'the name row is covered'}".
a boolean called 'the retag row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-07".
Print "VAL-07 asserts: {'the retag row is covered'}".
a boolean called 'the retype row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-09".
Print "VAL-09 asserts: {'the retype row is covered'}".
a boolean called 'the worked example row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-10".
Print "VAL-10 asserts: {'the worked example row is covered'}".
a boolean called 'the predicate row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-11".
Print "VAL-11 asserts: {'the predicate row is covered'}".
a boolean called 'the failed conversion row is covered' is 'gen text contains' of "ASSERT VAL-13".
Print "VAL-13 asserts: {'the failed conversion row is covered'}".

(The three constructs the section's own rules turn on, checked by needle
 rather than by leaf name: a value parameter, a value return, and the
 variable named `value` that LANGUAGE.md:2588 says must stay legal.)
a boolean called 'a value parameter appears' is 'gen text contains' of "with a value called".
Print "value parameter emitted: {'a value parameter appears'}".
a boolean called 'a value return appears' is 'gen text contains' of "Return a value,".
Print "value return emitted: {'a value return appears'}".
a boolean called 'the word value is a name' is 'gen text contains' of "a value is ".
Print "value used as a name: {'the word value is a name'}".

(The compiler comes from 'vox path', which honours $VOX the way test.sh
 sets it - a literal ../vox/... would only be right in a plain checkout
 and would silently skip the check in a worktree.)
a text called 'the compile command' is "{'shell quote' of 'vox path'} ./vf_values_a.vox -o ./vf_values_a > ./vf_values_a.cerr 2>&1".
a number called 'the compile status' is 'run shell' of 'the compile command' and 60000.
Print "the value leaves compile: {'the compile status' is 0}".
a number called 'the run status' is 'run shell' of "./vf_values_a > ./vf_values_a.out 2>&1" and 60000.
Print "every value assertion holds: {'the run status' is 0}".

(Only on failure, so the expected file is unchanged when this passes. A
 bare `0` on either line above is unreadable in a log from someone
 else's machine - which compiler it used and what the program actually
 said are the two facts needed to tell a real assertion failure from a
 mis-resolved path.)
If 'the compile status' is not 0 then,
    a text called 'the compiler complaint' is 'read whole file' of "./vf_values_a.cerr",
    Print "the compiler was {'vox path'}",
    Print 'the compiler complaint'.
If 'the run status' is not 0 then,
    a text called 'what the program printed' is 'read whole file' of "./vf_values_a.out",
    Print 'what the program printed'.
